            is associated with the progression of hepatocellular carcinoma
            (HCC). Further results suggest that miR103 promotes HCC metastasis
            and EMT by directly inhibiting LATS2.
REFERENCE   4  (bases 1 to 62)
  AUTHORS   Wang DS, Zhong B, Zhang MS and Gao Y.
  TITLE     Upregulation of serum miR-103 predicts unfavorable prognosis in
            patients with colorectal cancer
  JOURNAL   Eur Rev Med Pharmacol Sci 22 (14), 4518-4523 (2018)
   PUBMED   30058684
  REMARK    GeneRIF: Study results demonstrated that serum miR-103 was
            overexpressed in colorectal cancer (CRC) subjects, could
            differentiate CRC cases from controls with relatively high
            accuracy, and significantly correlated with worse clinical factors,
            as well as poorer recurrence-free survival or overall survival.
            Taken together, serum miR-103 might be a promising biomarker for
            diagnosis and prognosis of CRC.
